BT /F2 14 Tf 0.01 0.43 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 50 713.0 Tm (HISTORICAL CHRONOLOGY) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 679.0 Tm (Andrew S. Tanenbaum created MINIX in the 1980s as an educational Unix-like operating system) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 662.0 Tm (for teaching operating-system design. Its filesystem was deliberately small and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 645.0 Tm (inspectable, with inodes and data zones rather than a complex journal or pooled allocator.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 628.0 Tm (The original design also influenced the early Linux period, when Linux initially supported) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 611.0 Tm (MINIX media before ext-family filesystems superseded it.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 582.0 Tm (MINIX disk formats evolved through V1, V2 and V3. The revisions expanded directory name) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 565.0 Tm (lengths and representable zone addresses, while retaining a general Unix-like) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 548.0 Tm (directory-to-inode model. A document that describes only one MINIX version is insufficient) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 531.0 Tm (for identifying an arbitrary source image.) Tj ET
